    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i need some help troubleshooting a sublimation issue. we've been running heat transfers for polyester blends but the color vibrancy is way lower than it should be and the edges look blurry. we are currently at 200c for 40 seconds with 4 bar of pressure. any idea what might be causing the poor transfer or the bleeding around the edges?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The sublimation issue you're facing is likely due to the heat and pressure settings not being optimized for your polyester blend, which is a common problem when trying to achieve vibrant colors and sharp edges. The 200°C temperature and 40 seconds duration might not be sufficient for the dyes to fully penetrate the fibers, leading to lower color vibrancy. Additionally, the 4 bar of pressure could be too high, causing the edges to bleed or the transfer to spread beyond the intended area.\n\nTo improve the transfer quality, consider adjusting the temperature to around 220°C and reducing the pressure to 2-3 bar. This will help the dyes bond more effectively with the polyester fibers, resulting in better color vibrancy and sharper edges. Also, ensure that the transfer paper is properly aligned and that the substrate is clean and free of any oils or contaminants that could interfere with the transfer process.\n\nIf the issue persists, it might be worth checking the quality of the sublimation ink and the transfer paper used. Sometimes, using high-quality materials can significantly improve the results. Additionally, consider running a test transfer on a scrap piece of fabric to fine-tune the settings before applying them to your actual project.",
      "source": "target",
